# PATENT CLAIMS

**Claim 1.** A mechanical energy storage operating mechanism for circuit breakers, comprising:

a mounting frame;

an energy storage assembly disposed on the mounting frame, including a main spring having a first end fixedly connected to the mounting frame and a second end connected to a spring seat, the spring seat being slidably engaged with a guide shaft;

a drive motor mounted on the mounting frame and operatively connected to a speed-reduction gear train;

an energy storage shaft rotatably supported on the mounting frame and drivingly coupled to an output end of the speed-reduction gear train, the energy storage shaft carrying a ratchet wheel and a cam plate coaxially fixed thereto;

a pawl assembly pivotally mounted on the mounting frame and engaging the ratchet wheel to permit unidirectional rotation of the energy storage shaft during an energy storage stroke and to prevent reverse rotation thereof;

a closing cam follower operatively connected to the cam plate and linked to a closing spring release mechanism, such that rotation of the energy storage shaft compresses the main spring to a charged position, wherein the cam plate cooperates with a trip latch to releasably retain the energy storage shaft in the charged position;

a closing electromagnet and an opening electromagnet each mounted on the mounting frame and respectively coupled to the trip latch and to a tripping shaft, such that energization of the closing electromagnet releases the trip latch to permit the main spring to drive a closing linkage into a closed position, and energization of the opening electromagnet actuates the tripping shaft to release a separate opening spring to drive the closing linkage into an open position; and

a position indicator mechanically coupled to the energy storage shaft and configured to display an energy-stored state or a de-energized state of the main spring;

wherein the energy storage assembly, the drive motor, the speed-reduction gear train, and the pawl assembly are arranged coaxially about the energy storage shaft within a single housing to form an integrated compact module.

**Claim 2.** The mechanism according to claim 1, wherein the pawl assembly comprises a primary pawl and an auxiliary pawl arranged in a staggered configuration relative to teeth of the ratchet wheel, such that at any rotational position of the ratchet wheel, at least one of the primary pawl or the auxiliary pawl is engaged with a tooth thereof, thereby preventing backlash and reverse rotation of the energy storage shaft during interruption of the energy storage stroke.

**Claim 3.** The mechanism according to claim 1, further comprising a buffer device disposed between the closing linkage and the mounting frame, the buffer device comprising a buffer spring and a damping piston configured to absorb residual kinetic energy of the closing linkage at an end of a closing stroke or an opening stroke, thereby reducing mechanical impact on the mounting frame.

**Claim 4.** The mechanism according to claim 1, wherein the trip latch comprises a roller latch pivotally mounted on the mounting frame and biased into engagement with a latching surface of the cam plate by a latch spring, the roller latch defining a self-locking angle relative to the latching surface such that the trip latch remains in a locked position under a load exerted by the compressed main spring absent actuation by the closing electromagnet.

**Claim 5.** The mechanism according to claim 1, wherein the speed-reduction gear train comprises a plurality of intermeshing gears including a worm gear stage, the worm gear stage being configured to provide self-locking of the drive motor against reverse rotation induced by the main spring, thereby preventing back-driving of the drive motor during and after the energy storage stroke.

**Claim 6.** The mechanism according to claim 1, further comprising a manual energy storage mechanism including a manually operable crank handle detachably coupled to the energy storage shaft via a one-way clutch, the one-way clutch being configured to permit manual rotation of the energy storage shaft in the energy storage direction independent of operation of the drive motor, and to disengage automatically upon resumption of motor-driven rotation.

**Claim 7.** The mechanism according to claim 1, wherein the mounting frame comprises a unitary die-cast housing defining a plurality of integrally formed bearing bosses for rotatably supporting the energy storage shaft, the tripping shaft, and the pawl assembly, thereby reducing a component count and improving structural rigidity and assembly precision of the mechanism.

**Claim 8.** The mechanism according to claim 1, further comprising a microswitch mounted on the mounting frame and operatively coupled to the cam plate, the microswitch being configured to generate a stop signal to the drive motor upon the energy storage shaft reaching the charged position, and to generate a start signal to the drive motor upon release of the main spring, thereby enabling automatic recharging of the energy storage assembly.

**Claim 9.** The mechanism according to claim 1, wherein the closing linkage comprises a four-bar linkage including a main shaft lever, a connecting rod, and an output lever operatively connected to a circuit breaker contact shaft, the four-bar linkage being configured to convert rotational output of the energy storage shaft into a substantially linear closing motion of the circuit breaker contacts while maintaining a mechanical advantage sufficient to overcome contact resistance forces during the closing stroke.

**Claim 10.** The mechanism according to claim 1, wherein all rotating shafts of the mechanism, including the energy storage shaft and the tripping shaft, are supported by self-lubricating bearings, and wherein the mounting frame further comprises a sealed enclosure portion configured to isolate the speed-reduction gear train and the pawl assembly from ambient dust and moisture, thereby enhancing long-term operational reliability of the mechanism.
